  "Source": "J. A. Dobrowolski, \"Optical Interference Filters for the Adjustment of Spectral Response and Spectral Power Distribution,\" Appl. Opt. 9, 1396-1402 (1970). doi:10.1364/AO.9.001396 - Table I (Filter B), p. 1400; Sec. III.B and Fig. 6, p. 1399",
  "Notes": "Four-layer component filter B of the constant-output-monochromator correction filter (5 filters B in series with 15 filters A, Sec. III.B)\nSpecification: See Dobrowolski1970-filterA: the series system 15A + 5B should follow the dotted curve of Fig. 6 (relative shape), 0.3-0.7 um.\nReported: No values for filter B alone; the 15A + 5B system result is plotted in Fig. 6 (see Dobrowolski1970-filterA reported).\nNotes: Incident medium air (Table I medium 1.000), substrate 1.450. Table lists Substrate, layers 1..N, Medium; layer 1 is next to the substrate (the synthesis program grows the design from the bare substrate), so the order is REVERSED here (layer 1 below = last printed layer, next to the medium). Thicknesses are printed as optical thicknesses n*d in micrometres; they are entered as FWOT at reference_nm = 1000 nm, which is the same number (1 FWOT at 1000 nm = 1 um optical thickness), with constant, non-dispersive, lossless indices exactly as printed. Physical thickness d = OT/n. Normal incidence assumed (not stated). 4 layers, as stated in the text; summed optical thickness of the printed layers = 0.5457 um (no total is printed).\nLayers from Table I (Filter B), p. 1400; Sec. III.B and Fig. 6, p. 1399; layer 1 faces the incident medium.\nNot checked: the paper reports this design only as plots.",
